---

<Page
    lang="en"
    enPath="/best-keepass-app-iphone/"
    canonicalPath="/best-keepass-app-iphone/"
    title={title}
    description={description}
    eyebrow="Buyer's guide"
    heading="The best KeePass app for iPhone"
    lead="There is no official KeePass app for iPhone, so you pick a compatible client. We compared five current apps on what you pay for, whether you can read the code, and what works in AutoFill."
>
    <aside class="disclosure">
        <p>
            <strong>Who wrote this:</strong> we make KeeForge, one of the apps below. To keep it useful anyway, every fact comes from the vendor’s own pages, linked under Sources, we list where each app beats ours, and we left out claims we couldn’t check. Facts checked on {checked}. If something is wrong or out of date, <a href="mailto:support@keeforge.com">tell us</a> and we’ll fix it.
        </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>The short answer</h2>
        <p>The right app depends on what you care about most. All five open the same KeePass <code class="mono">.kdbx</code> files, so you can try more than one on the same database.</p>
        <div class="pick-grid">
            <div class="pick-card">
                <h3>You want an independently audited app</h3>
                <p><strong>KeePassium.</strong> The only one here with a published third-party code audit (Cure53, 2024). Free for one database.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="pick-card">
                <h3>You want the most features</h3>
                <p><strong>Strongbox.</strong> Password Safe files, Apple Watch, a duress PIN and the widest sync list. Face ID unlock and offline editing need Pro.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="pick-card">
                <h3>You want everything free and open source</h3>
                <p><strong>KeeForge</strong> (iPhone, iPad and Mac) or <strong>OneKeePass</strong> (also Android, Windows and Linux). No paid tier and no database limit in either.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="pick-card">
                <h3>You’d rather pay once</h3>
                <p><strong>PanicVault.</strong> A single $4.99 purchase covers iPhone, iPad and Mac. Closed source, KDBX 4 only.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
        <p class="vs-table-note">Checked {checked} against each vendor’s website, App Store listing and source repository. Prices are US App Store or vendor website prices and can differ by country. Swipe sideways on a phone.</p>
        <div class="vs-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="KeePass apps for iPhone compared">
            <table class="vs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Feature</span></th>
                        {apps.map((app) => <th scope="col">{app}</th>)}
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {rows.map((row) => (
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                            {row.cells.map((cell) => <td>{cell}</td>)}
                        </tr>
                    ))}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section apps">
        <h2>Each app in a paragraph</h2>

        <article class="app-note">
            <h3>KeePassium</h3>
            <p>
                The established choice. KeePassium is open source under GPLv3, and it is the only app here that has paid for an independent audit: Cure53 reviewed the iOS app and its cryptography in October 2024, and the developer says every finding was fixed in the next update. The free version is capable (sync, AutoFill, Face ID, 2FA codes) but limited to one database; YubiKey and Quick AutoFill are paid. Its Mac app is built with Mac Catalyst. Its App Store build was last updated in November 2025, though newer 2.6 versions are tagged in its source repository.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="app-note">
            <h3>Strongbox</h3>
            <p>
                The most feature-rich. Strongbox also opens Password Safe files, runs on Apple Watch and Vision Pro, and connects directly to the longest list of storage services, including SFTP. The free version includes AutoFill, passkeys, 2FA codes and key files on iPhone, but Face ID / Touch ID unlock and offline editing are Pro features, which many people will want. Strongbox was acquired by Applause in March 2025. Its code is published under AGPL-3.0, but the project itself calls it “source available” rather than open source, and the published code trails the App Store releases. Strongbox says it has not had an independent code-level audit.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="app-note">
            <h3>PanicVault</h3>
            <p>
                The simple one-time purchase. PanicVault costs $4.99 once on the US App Store, with no subscription or in-app purchases, and covers iPhone, iPad, Mac and Vision Pro. It supports key files and YubiKey over NFC on iPhone and USB on Mac. It is closed source, opens KDBX 4 databases only, syncs directly with iCloud Drive and Google Drive, and is available in English only. PanicVault publishes its own KeePass-for-iPhone guide, which is worth reading alongside this one.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="app-note">
            <h3>OneKeePass</h3>
            <p>
                The cross-platform open-source option. OneKeePass is free and GPLv3, with apps for iPhone, iPad and Android and desktop apps for Mac, Windows and Linux, so it suits you if your devices aren’t all Apple. On iPhone it AutoFills passwords, 2FA codes and passkeys, and connects directly to SFTP and WebDAV. It is still pre-1.0 (version 0.25), English only, and doesn’t document YubiKey support.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="app-note">
            <h3>KeeForge</h3>
            <p>
                Our app. KeeForge is free, open source under GPLv3, and has no account, subscription, paid tier or database limit. It runs on iPhone, iPad and Mac, where 1.23 rebuilt it as a native Mac app. It reads and writes KDBX 4, opens KDBX 3.1 read-only, and AutoFills passwords, passkeys and 2FA codes. On iPhone and iPad it connects directly to Dropbox, OneDrive, WebDAV and FTP and opens files from iCloud Drive and other Files providers; on Mac it uses WebDAV, FTP and synced folders. Where it falls short: it needs iOS 18, YubiKey databases open read-only and only on iPhone over NFC, it has no Google Drive connection yet, it is newer and smaller than KeePassium or Strongbox, and it has not had an independent security audit.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>Coming from KeePassXC?</h2>
        <p>
            KeePassXC has no iPhone app. Its FAQ says the team doesn’t make a mobile app and suggests Strongbox and KeePassium on iOS. Any app in this guide opens a KeePassXC database, as long as it is KDBX 4 (KeePassXC’s default). If you store passkeys in KeePassXC, KeeForge, KeePassium and PanicVault all read KeePassXC’s passkey format.
        </p>
        <p>
            To use the same database on your computer and your iPhone, put the file somewhere both can reach, such as iCloud Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive or a WebDAV server, then add it in the iPhone app. Check that the app you choose connects to the service you already use.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>Head-to-head comparisons</h2>
        <p>Want more detail on KeeForge against a single app?</p>
        <ul class="link-list">
            <li><a href="/vs/keepassium/">KeeForge vs KeePassium</a></li>
            <li><a href="/vs/strongbox/">KeeForge vs Strongbox</a></li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-section">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <p>Opened and checked on {checked}. App versions and dates are from the US App Store.</p>
        <div class="sources">
            {sources.map((source) => (
                <div class="source-group">
                    <h3>{source.group}</h3>
                    <ul>
                        {source.links.map((link) => <li><a href={link.href} rel="noopener">{link.label}</a></li>)}
                    </ul>
                </div>
            ))}
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vs-cta">
        <h2>Try KeeForge</h2>
        <p>Free, open source, no account.</p>
        <div class="cta-row">
            <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6759309295" class="app-store-badge" aria-label="Download on the App Store">
                <img src="https://tools.applemediaservices.com/api/badges/download-on-the-app-store/black/en-us?size=250x83" alt="Download on the App Store" height="52">
            </a>
            <a href="/" class="ghost-link">Back to homepage</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</Page>

